<http://localhost:5002/6ab65cad-b213-4ddd-b013-f8255d889df2> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/ns/dcat#Dataset>.
<http://localhost:5002/6ab65cad-b213-4ddd-b013-f8255d889df2> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> <https://doi.org/10.25835%2Fwzrn3izc>.
<http://localhost:5002/6ab65cad-b213-4ddd-b013-f8255d889df2> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/title> "CT-based porosity analysis of 316L/CuCrZr multi-material specimens".
<http://localhost:5002/6ab65cad-b213-4ddd-b013-f8255d889df2> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/description> "This dataset was generated in the context of the parameter study presented in the paper: _Additive manufacturing of multi-material parts – Influence of process parameters on interface formation in in-plane 316L/CuCrZr laser powder bed fusion_  It contains the __VGSTUDIO MAX porosity analysis reports__ for all analyzed specimens. Porosity analysis was performed in VGSTUDIO MAX (version 2026.1). Each reconstructed volume was first registered to the nominal CAD geometry by best-fit alignment to provide a common coordinate system. Pores were then quantified within a __9.3 × 9.3 × 1.5 mm³__ region of interest centered on the coordinate origin of each sample, using the __VGEasyPore__ algorithm (Porosity/Inclusion Analysis module) in relative mode with a minimum contrast of 20% and a local area size of 10 voxels. The built-in noise reduction was set to \\'Low\\', and indications below a minimum size of 8 voxels were discarded to suppress image artifacts. The reports include the total porosity, pore statistics, indication tables, three-dimensional pore visualizations, and the complete analysis settings.  The dataset contains porosity analysis reports for the following specimen numbers (ordered by increasing volumetric energy density): 8, 16, 31, 1, 24, 26, 33, 15 (3×), 40, 10, 32, 25.  ![ Representative CT-based porosity analysis of specimen 8.](https://data.uni-hannover.de/dataset/2fe1feea-710f-4b49-9678-c411a454e1a5/resource/2873b591-b79d-4e35-9a53-449eabf4624c/download/representive_specimen_8.png) Representative CT-based porosity analysis of specimen 8.  ### References  Meyer, I., Oel, M., Ehlers, T., & Lachmayer, R. (2023). *Additive manufacturing of multi-material parts – Design guidelines for manufacturing of 316L/CuCrZr in laser powder bed fusion*. Heliyon, 9(8), e18301. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.heliyon.2023.e18301  Ina Meyer, Roland Lachmayer (2025). *Additive manufacturing data from a parameter study on 316L/CuCrZr multi-material specimens* [Data set]. LUIS. https://doi.org/10.25835/7p6wv6cg  Ina Meyer, Roland Lachmayer (2025). *Light microscopy data from a parameter study on 316L/CuCrZr multi-material specimens* [Data set]. LUIS. https://doi.org/10.25835/rsjz3w2k  Ina Meyer, Roland Lachmayer (2025). *SEM data from a parameter study on 316L/CuCrZr multi-material specimens* [Data set]. LUIS. https://doi.org/10.25835/wan2sabi  ### Acknowledgments  The project ”*Additive Fertigung multimaterieller Komponenten zur nachhaltigen Energiewandlung*” was funded by the European Regional Development Fund (ERDF) and the state of Lower Saxony – Project number ZW 7-87011865.  The project ”*Major Research Instrumentation for integration of efficient effects in multi-material structural components*” was funded by the Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft (DFG - German Research Foundation) - Project number 445707542.  We would like to thank Volume Graphics GmbH for providing the VGSTUDIO MAX software, which was used to analyze the CT scans in this study.".
